  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Becoming blustery and cold tonight into Friday \nwith mountain flurries and snow showers.\n\nAn upper-level low continues to track through the interior Mid-\nAtlantic before moving into the southern Ohio Valley by Friday \nmorning. While the first phase of rain has departed, the upper-\nlevel low itself has pushed a second round of precipitation and\ngusty west-southwest winds mainly into the mountains overnight.\nBelow 3,500 feet, this moisture has started as rain showers and\ndrizzle, becoming a rain and snow mix, while higher elevations \nwill see mostly light snow. As colder air filters in throughout \nthe day on Friday, all lingering precipitation will transition \ninto light snow or flurries. Warm ground temperatures at lower \nelevations will prevent snow from accumulating on road surfaces,\nthough the highest peaks will pick up an inch or two. While \nFriday's winds will not match the intensity of earlier gusts, \nthey will remain strong enough to require securing loose outdoor\nitems. Blustery conditions and below-normal temperatures will \nkeep wind chills in the 20s across the mountains and the 30s \nacross the foothills and Piedmont on Friday, before warmer, \ndrier weather finally returns for the weekend.",
